INT. NIGHT FERRY - OBSERVATION LOUNGE - NIGHT

Empty vinyl seats. A vending machine glowing blue. Black water slides past the windows. The engine thrums like a cat.

CLEMENTINE ABIODUN-WRIGHT (30s) sits with a paperback open and unread on her knee. She has a coat draped over her shoulders. She keeps turning the same page.

ISAAC VANDERHOEK-MWANGI (30s) comes in from the deck with two paper cups. He stops at the door. He lifts one cup in an offer.

She looks up. She nods.

He crosses the lounge. Sits one seat away. Hands her a cup. She takes it. Their fingers do not touch.

CLEMENTINE
Thanks.

ISAAC
It's terrible.

CLEMENTINE
I figured.

She sips. Grimaces. He grins at his own cup.

ISAAC
(to himself)
Nine years.

CLEMENTINE
Sorry?

ISAAC
Nothing. Just counting.

She sets the book on the seat between them. He glances at it. Reads the spine. She notices.

CLEMENTINE
You've read it.

ISAAC
You gave it to me.

CLEMENTINE
Did I?

ISAAC
Third floor, Lincoln Street. You said it would change my life.

CLEMENTINE
Did it?

ISAAC
Not really.

She laughs. A real one, quick and surprised. He watches her do it.

CLEMENTINE
(to the empty room)
All right, ferry. All right.

ISAAC
Who are you talking to?

CLEMENTINE
The whole boat. It's been listening all night.

He looks at the empty seats. At the black windows. At her.

ISAAC
Then let's give it something to hear.

She reaches for the book. Opens it to the page she was stuck on. Hands it to him.

CLEMENTINE
Read me this part.

He takes it. Begins, quietly. She listens, head tilted, cup warm in both hands.
